 the end of this deck there are also  some useful links that provide  supplemental information we also have  some technical  terms and definitions in the end  so we'll start with an overview of the  three technologies  let's first review the next gen  sequencing first  so both whole genome sequencing and low  pass sequencing are based on shotgun  sequencing  the entire genome is broken up into  small fragments of dna as shown in the  left panel  these fragments are then sequenced and  then assembled together using a  reference genome  as shown in the right panel there may be  multiple reads that align to the same  region of the reference sequence while  other regions may not have any reads at  all  there are four factors to consider when  planning a next-gen sequencing  experiment  the cost per sample  the sequencing reads per sample  the accuracy of  calls based on the research goal  and then the sample throughput  the whole genome sequencing is typically  done at a very high coverage of over 20  x between 20 to 30 x meaning that every  loci in a genome you get the uh on an  average has 20 weeks and with 20 leads  you can reliably make a call  the level of coverage can be adjusted by  multiplexing samples so if more samples  are multiplexed together then each  sample will receive fewer sequence reads  and this will in turn impact the  genotyping accuracy  as you can see from the illustration on  the left the low coverage sequencing  reads are shown in pink so the regions  have fewer overlapping reads and there  are also gaps in sequence  where there are no reads present  so multiplexing does reduce the cost per  sample and increases the sample  throughput but it does that at the  expense of genotyping accuracy  the fewer reads will impact the  confidence of making the genotyping  calls and can result in false positive  or false negative calls  the low pass sequencing is uh performed  at coverage between 0.1 x and 4x  and predictive genomics applications are  typically carried out between 0.5 x and  1 x  so the experiment starts with  multiplexing hundreds of samples  together on a single flow cell  followed by a sequencing at 0.5 x to 1 x  then the reads are aligned to the  reference genome and variant calling is  performed to get the genotype  likelihoods  then the mutation is carried out  to refine the genotype calls and impute  the missing genotypes  so in the end all all you get are  imputed genotypes and no direct  genotypes  so imputation has reasonable power for  common and low frequency variants but  not for rare variants  so a red variants are challenging to  impute and most  more often  the red variants are missing from the  reference panels and they will not be  genotyped at all  so different applications may require  different depths of coverage  in this graphic i'm showing on one end  lps where you multiplex a lot of samples  to achieve coverage between 0.1 x and 4x  this results in low cost per sample but  at the price of genotyping quality  on the other end of the spectrum you  have deep whole genome sequencing at 20  x coverage  you get very high confidence  stereotyping calls but at the cost but  the cost per sample is high  i will discuss axiom microarrays in the  next slide but as shown in the table  below the cost of access microarrays  falls somewhere between 0.5 x to 1 x  so the third technology in our  introduction is microarrays  this is not the sequencing technology  instead it uses hybridization  so the glass surface of microarray is  divided into  square features  each of which contains many copies of  specific dna probe sequence  as shown in the first graphic the  different features have different probes  represented in blue and green probes in  the adjacent features  as shown in the second graphic the probe  sequences  are carefully designed to hybridize a  specific target sequence containing the  genetic variant that exists in the  population  probes are designed for each allele of a  gradient and microarray is able to  capture either or both at least if they  are present in a sample  so once the samples are a sample has  been hybridized dna is fluorescently  labeled and then scanned by the laser  and the fluorescent signal confirms  which alien is present at which target  locus  this light highlights uh the axiom  microarray design  capabilities what differentiates exam  microarrays is the content customization  to meet diverse research needs  so with the help of proprietary array  design and data analysis  axiom  algorithms  can address even the most difficult and  great markers  we have imputation design pipeline that  can optimally select markers from  next-gen sequencing data to provide  high accuracy and coverage for the  population of interest  we also have a proprietary database of  11 million markers that have been wet  lab tested and this ensures very high  quality with small  space we also offer a modular design  where customers can start from our  catalog pre-made arrays or customize  the appropriate content representing  their target population  so in terms of genotyping accuracy uh  acid microarrays have shown to deliver  high genome-wide accuracy as well as  very uh accurate genotypes of specific  variants  and that in a very cost effective manner  so the jiva's backbone on population  arrays comprises of very highly  informative markers which are selected  by taking into account ld patterns in a  population  to achieve very high imputation coverage  in the population of interest  in addition to the high genome wide  accuracy  axiom arrays also show high accuracy of  calling specific variants  so using specialized algorithms and  probe design capabilities we can  accurately genotype  complex yet important variants  um we've shown that axiom arrays deliver  very high positive predictive value for  rare variants with a leaf frequency less  than one percent  then we can also address  complex genomic regions such as  pharmacogenes that suffer from high  homology frequent copy number changes  and low sequence complexity  um finally um axiom areas also show high  accuracy for genotyping indels as well  as copy number variations which are  usually difficult to genotype using  next-gen sequencing applications  now that we have looked at each of the  technologies let's compare them side by  side  whole genome sequencing prioritizes data  quality at the expense of high cost per  sample and look sample throughput it's a  very powerful  uh  tool for de novo variant discovery  across the entire map range and the  genotypes can be called directly from  the sequence data for common low  frequency as well as red variants  however the data analysis for whole  genome sequencing is quite complex  low pass sequencing on the other hand  prioritizes  lower cost per sample and high sample  throughput at the ex at the expense of  sequence data quality  so it relies on imputation for genotype  calling and as a result uh it has  reasonable power for common and low  frequency variants but not for the red  variants  um  low coverage also makes calling of  variance in regions such as high  homology and frequent copy number  changes  and  sequences with the low sequence  complexity very difficult  so low pass sequencing can only genotype  the variants that are present in the  imputation reference panel  and in addition of that  with low pass sequencing you also need  to do  data qc and alignment um as well as  imputation which requires very high  level of bioinformatics expertise  compared to next-gen sequencing  microarrays have very complementary set  of capabilities  so probe hybridization is very specific  and it enables high quality direct  genotypes for rare as well as common and  low frequency variants  and the throughput can be scaled from  less than 100 samples to hundreds and  thousands of samples  um  then  just like low pass sequencing  microarrays can be used for  genome-wide imputation of millions of  common and low frequency variants  but unlike  low pass sequencing the data analysis uh  is  much lower complexity  one disadvantage of microarrays is that  it cannot be used for denoble variant  discovery  and the selection of variants for array  design needs to be done carefully to  minimize any ascertainment bias  so  we just discussed whole genome  sequencing low pass sequencing and  microarrays offer very complementary  capabilities now  next let's see how these can be  integrated together in predictive  genomics research  so predictive genomics aims to predict a  disease risk and understand the drug  response in order to improve the  future of health outcomes and there are  three main applications of predictive  genomics  population scale genotyping for diseases  prediction through the polygenic risk  scores  then pharmacogenomics which involves  tailoring medication and dosage based on  individuals genetic profile  and finally the third is carrier  screening to understand the risk factors  for broad range of inherited  mendelian disorders  predictive genomics is going to be the  future of health care and in order to  make it mainstream it's very important  to accurately and affordably genotype  variants that are associated with  complex traits and diseases  so the next part of this presentation  we will look at each application  and challenges associated with them and  explore the technology strategies for  each of them  first let's look at the population scale  genotyping for diseases risk prediction  through polygenic risk scores  so polygenic risk scores combine the  contribution of multiple genetic loci  most commonly common variants with small  effects  and the goal is to estimate the  individual's likelihood for a disease or  a trait  the recent studies have also highlighted  the importance of rare variants in  predicting the risk for common complex  diseases  so in order to support polygenic risk or  studies  the technology should be able to support  large-scale population-wide screening at  a low cost  um unfortunately low-pass sequencing  the whole genome sequencing cannot  support it whereas both low-pass  sequencing and microarrays can support  this  then it should be also  be able to support high genome wide  accuracy across diverse populations  which all three technologies can deliver  one challenge as i mentioned is rare  variants  as the imputation of rail variants has a  limited accuracy low pass has limited  accuracy for  for polygenic risk scores calculations  that involve red variance  on the other hand uh  their variants can be accurately  genotyped by directly typing them on the  axiom arrays  next let uh let's look at  pharmacogenomics  so pharmacogenomics involves uh  tailoring the medication and dosage  based on individuals genetic profile  and for pharmacogenomics to be used in  personalized medicine  very high you know genotyping accuracy  is needed  the important pharmacogenomics genes  such as hla c2b6 these are regions with  very high  polymorphisms  they also have complex structures  and high sequence homology and are very  difficult to genotype  as i've shown  in the example below the sip2d60  it's involved in the metabolism of over  25 drugs that are on the market today  uh if you have a pathogenic variant in  this gene then it can impact the  efficacy of some of the drugs  cyp2d6 is a very complex gene and very  challenging to genotype  it has high sequence similarity with the  septude7 silver gene and undergoes copy  number variations and gene arrangements  with sip to d7  so any short read next gen sequencing  cannot deal with such complex genomic  regions  it requires very high coverage more than  20 x for accurate genotyping  so low pass sequencing  cannot accurately genotype these regions  on the other hand  uh i said my microarrays can accurately  genotype these regions  by using complex probe designs  uh  some targeted amplification and also  using advanced algorithms  so the final application we 