tected by  arrays  so that's the end of the webinar uh  here's a link for some resources that  are available from thermofisher there's  a white paper available  then there are also a few case studies  that scientists introduced on thermo  fisher predictive genomics landing page  thank you for your attention  i think you are new for your informative  presentation we will now start the live  q a portion of this webinar if you have  a question you'd like to ask please do  so now just click on the ask a question  box located on the far left of your  screen we'll answer as many of these  questions as we have time for  okay let's get started our first  question here is  are there any precision medicine  initiatives where microarrays have been  used  good question yeah there have been  several uh large-scale precision  medicine efforts that have used uh axiom  arrays  and uh just to mention a few um  there's a taiwan precision medicine  array where the goal is to genotype one  million taiwanese individuals  then there's fringe and study who are  using axial platform and there the goal  is to genotype 10 of their population  there's also med genome project in india  so all these studies are geared towards  managing health and prevention of  diseases in their population  and i also like to mention  the university of pittsburgh medical  center upmc uh they're using our  pharmacogenomics platform  and they're looking to offer preemptive  pharmacogenomics testing to over  uh four billion patients that are  enrolled into upmc healthcare system  okay  it's a new  uh let's move on to question two  can lps be combined with targeted panels  to address some of the challenges  related to pharmacogenomics  and rare variants  yes definitely so um  there are targeted next-gen sequencing  panels which offer small number of genes  or specific variants these can be  combined with low pass sequencing  so the targeted panels are usually run  at very high coverage  and  they can be used to address uh  pharmacogenomics and rare variants that  i mentioned are challenging with  low carbohydrate sequencing  um but the drawback is that if you  combine a low pass with the targeted  sequencing then they no longer  remain cost effective for large  population initiatives  um in addition to that the workflow also  becomes cumbersome  uh and that includes both the lab  workflow as well as the bioinformatics  analysis combining the data from um  targeted panels with the low pass  sequencing  so um i i'd like to mention that  microarrays provide a single solution  you can genotype these targeted uh  regions as well as uh  get very high genome wide accuracy  uh at the same time  thanks for now uh looking  looks like we have one more question  here  pull it up uh  how are the population arrays designed  and what imputation coverage can be  achieved  using axiom arrays  right so again a good question um so the  population arrays have a g g bus  backbone uh which is selected using our  proprietary educational marker selection  algorithm  and the markers are selected  so that they efficiently cover both  common and low frequency variation in  multiple populations  and to ensure high performance as well  as optimize  the use of space on the arrays  the markers are selected or the probe  sets are selected from a pool of our 10  million high performing experimentally  verified probe sets  um so we can leverage uh the thousand  genomes reference panel or any other  custom reference panel for the design  and we take into account the ld  architecture in all populations  simultaneously  uh to achieve equivalent imputation  power in each population  so to give some sense on reputation  coverage um that that we can achieve  so with thousand genomes reference panel  uh with roughly eight hundred thousand  markers  we expect to achieve uh imputation  accuracy of  over 93 to 95 percent for common markers  and for the low frequency markers with  the map range between one to five  perc